Most independent businesses spend their marketing effort on finding new customers. But the people most likely to spend money with you next week are the ones who already have. They know where you are, they liked what they got, and they trust you.

Getting them to come back a little more often is usually the cheapest and quickest way to grow. Here are nine ways to do it.

## Why do repeat customers matter so much?

A new customer has to discover you, decide to try you and make a special trip. A returning customer only has to remember you. That's why small improvements in how often people come back add up so quickly.

{% callout tone="example" %}
Say 200 different customers visit your café each week, spending £6 each time. If just 20 of them came back one extra time a week, that's £120 more a week, or about £6,000 a year, without a single new customer.
{% /callout %}

## 1. Make the first visit worth remembering

People come back to places that made them feel welcome. Learn regulars' names and usual orders. Thank first-timers for trying you. Small extras, like a taste of something new or a tip about the area, cost little and get talked about.

## 2. Give people a reason to come back before they leave

The best moment to win a second visit is at the end of the first. Before someone walks out, give them something that points to next time:

- a stamp on their loyalty card;
- a next-visit offer, such as a free pastry with their next coffee;
- an invitation to follow you, so they hear about new offers.

Without a reason, even happy customers drift. With one, coming back becomes the natural next step.

## 3. Run a loyalty card that's easy to finish

Loyalty cards work when the reward feels reachable. "Buy 9 coffees, get the 10th free" works because a regular can finish it in a couple of weeks. "Spend £200 and get £5 off" feels like homework.

A few rules of thumb:

- **Keep it short.** Six to ten visits suits most cafés, barbers and takeaways.
- **Make the reward something people want**, not leftovers or slow sellers.
- **Give a head start.** A card that starts with one stamp already filled tends to get finished more often than an empty one.

Paper cards get lost and are easy to fake, so many businesses now use a digital card on the customer's phone. We compare the two in [digital loyalty cards vs paper stamp cards](/blog/digital-vs-paper-loyalty-cards).

## 4. Ask happy customers to follow you

A customer who follows you can hear about your next offer. One who doesn't is relying on memory. Ask at the moment people are happiest: when you hand over their order or finish their appointment.

Make it one tap, not a form. A QR code by the till that lets people follow you and join your loyalty card at the same time works well.

## 5. Use quiet times to bring people back

Most businesses have hours when staff are waiting for customers. Those are the perfect times to invite regulars back, with an offer that only runs then. You fill a gap that was costing you money, and customers get a reason to visit at a time that suits them.

We go into this in detail in [how to fill quiet hours](/blog/how-to-fill-quiet-hours).

## 6. Reward your regulars, not just newcomers

Many businesses offer their best deals to strangers and nothing to the people who visit every week. Flip it round. Give regulars early access to new products, a birthday treat, or a surprise free item now and then. They notice, and they tell their friends.

## 7. Ask for reviews at the right moment

Reviews bring new customers, but asking also deepens the relationship with existing ones. Ask right after a good experience, such as after someone redeems a loyalty reward, and make it one tap to your Google review page.

## 8. Remember birthdays and milestones

A small birthday treat is one of the most effective reasons to bring someone back, because it feels personal rather than promotional. Collect birthdays (day and month only) when people sign up, and send the treat automatically so you don't have to remember.

## 9. Count who comes back

You can't improve what you can't see. At the very least, count:

- how many customers came back at least once in the last month;
- how many loyalty rewards were earned;
- which offers brought people in.

If you're using paper cards and a till, this is guesswork. A digital loyalty card and offer system counts it for you.

